{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat strategy_validation_feedback.json strategy_validation_feedback.md VENUE.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md; cat paper_strategy.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"generated_at\": \"2026-11-12T23:59:09.224847+00:00\",\n  \"hard_errors\": [\n    \"paper_strategy.json supplementary_plan.planned_subsections[2].source_ids must be a non-empty list\"\n  ],\n  \"ok\": false,\n  \"paper_dir\": \"work/results/r03/codex/20261112_215151/paper\"\n}# Paper strategy validation feedback\n\n- generated_at: 2026-11-12T23:59:09.224847+00:00\n- paper_dir: `work/results/r03/codex/20261112_215151/paper`\n- ok: `False`\n\n## Hard errors\n- paper_strategy.json supplementary_plan.planned_subsections[2].source_ids must be a non-empty list\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n---\nname: write-paper-standalone\ndescription: Write a grounded LaTeX paper as one coherent standalone scientific study centered on a single defensible thesis rather than the run chronology.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate `paper.tex` and `claims_grounding.json` from grounded artifacts only, with a clean standalone-paper narrative.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper step when the default paper-writing skill keeps drifting into a search diary.\n- Need a standalone scientific paper built around one central method, hypothesis, or comparison story.\n- Need to hide run order and expose only the subset of baselines and ablations that support a coherent grounded manuscript.\n\n# What to do\n1. Read:\n- `results_readme.md` — campaign-level overview of all tasks, variants, and where to find results. Use this to understand the full scope of the campaign beyond the primary task passed via `--task-dir`.\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n23. Keep visible paper text free of internal pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or `rubric`.\n24. If you need a thresholded rate, name it with one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment rate, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse one label for different task-specific thresholds.\n25. When `paper_data.json.ablation_studies` is non-empty, prefer its variant summaries, replicate statistics, and explicit comparisons over inferring ablations from `all_programs` alone.\n26. Use only numbers present in `paper_data.json`.\n27. Use only citation keys present in `references.bib`.\n28. Use `global_memory_summary.md` only as private qualitative framing; do not use it for quantitative claims or `source_ids`.\n29. If `paper_strategy.md` and `paper_strategy.json` are present, treat them as the framing contract for the final draft; fail clearly if they contradict grounded evidence.\n30. Keep visible paper text free of run-diary wording such as discovery order, iteration order, or agent-loop narration.\n31. Write claim mapping JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"text\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"SomeKey2024\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n32. Run validator:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis validator also checks that `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` stay in sync, and traces every `@evidence[[...]]` link under `--paper-dir` against the `scientific-evidence/` bundle via the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier. Every link must classify as `resolved` or `anchored`; any `dangling` link is a hard error you must fix before finalization. If paper text carries `@evidence[[...]]` tokens but the bundle is missing, generate it first with `bash ../scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir .`.\n33. During repair passes, also rerun the full `validate-paper` workflow after each fix round so the next round starts from fresh feedback, not stale assumptions.\n34. When venue guidance is present, make the title, abstract, introduction, and conclusion explicitly reader-facing for that venue's scientific questions whenever the grounded evidence supports that framing.\n35. If `./paper_authors.tex` exists, treat it as the canonical author source. Keep `\\input{paper_authors.tex}` inside `\\author{...}` when possible; otherwise copy the exact file contents without edits.\n36. Keep the template's watermark block (`% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---` … `% --- end watermark ---`) in `paper.tex` unchanged; the orchestrator regenerates it before every host compile.\n\n# Outputs\n- `paper.tex`\n- `claims_grounding.json`\n- `figure_manifest.json` when figures are generated or required by strategy\n- Optional extra figures in `figures/` (grounded only, no cross-agent aggregate plots).\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Missingness-Aware Latent Models for Budgeted Embedding Benchmark Prediction\",\n  \"central_question\": \"How accurately can a historical-only, missingness-aware latent-model procedure predict an unseen embedding model’s recorded but unqueried benchmark scores at small query budgets, and how does this change under name-proxy-family-disjoint evaluation and destroyed historical cross-task alignment?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"On the evaluated English MTEB panels, the frozen latent-model procedure has lower aggregate prediction error than the evaluated random-acquisition low-rank and greedy-coverage ridge pipelines, including on never-queryable audit targets, but the advantage is not uniform across held-out splits and does not isolate the contribution of mixtures or value-adaptive acquisition.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The strongest evidence is the completed final evaluation and its row/checkpoint records, not the search ordering or unverified internal diagnostics: these support direct error-versus-budget comparisons, common-target audits, an adverse family-disjoint split, and a historical-column permutation control. One shared question covers two split conditions on a single pinned embedding benchmark source; these are not independent datasets. The paper is an empirical method-integration study with explicit limits, not a new-architecture claim, a universal benchmark-compression result, or a campaign report. Final qualification is independently reported as completed/qualified by final.qualification.status and final.qualification.outcome; that administrative outcome is not evidence of universal validity, superiority, or mechanistic attribution.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Not an edit-mode run: previous_paper, staged new/old programs, aggregated, extra_tasks, contributions.md, and instantiated edit-context files are absent. Rejected means no inherited manuscript story is adopted, not that an existing manuscript was evaluated and rejected. Preserve-the-prior-story is inapplicable; broadening the source brief to generative language models is unsupported; recentering on the latest quadrature branch is unsupported. Select the frozen evaluated latent-model family, with the two split conditions as evidence blocks under one thesis.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No staged manuscript or edit-context payload was found; literal placeholder strings do not enable edit mode.\",\n    \"paper_data.json has no downstream_study and no study.* grounding sources. Do not invent campaign-selection statistics, original-incumbent comparisons, scored/unscored counts, or search-score improvements; do not substitute result.* sources for the requested study.* facts.\",\n    \"Private provenance only: results/best_program_manifest.json points to the latent-mixture source directory, not the quadrature extension. Describe the frozen evaluated procedure, including its Gaussian fallback; do not infer the actually chosen component count or selector from code availability.\",\n    \"The final evidence contains model-group and name-proxy-family-disjoint conditions, original/permuted historical-score arms, and a zero-weight reset replay. Those measured conditions set the scope, rather than the broader generative-model aspiration in the original brief.\",\n    \"ablation_studies is empty and candidate_diagnostics_trust is unverified. Static code explains available mechanisms but cannot establish which internal alternative ran or caused the measured advantage.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Historical-only missingness-aware latent prediction and transfer selection\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The frozen studied procedure fits observed-data low-rank-plus-diagonal covariance, considers a small latent-mixture extension, conditions on revealed scores, and uses historical fixed-target validation with a Gaussian fallback. Present it as one validation-selected procedure, not separately measured component methods.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Random acquisition with masked low-rank prediction\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"A genuinely executed comparison with explicit per-row/checkpoint records and final MAE; it changes both fitting and acquisition relative to the proposed procedure, so it is not an isolated acquisition ablation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Greedy correlation-coverage acquisition with ridge prediction\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The stronger evaluated comparator uses overlap-shrunk historical correlations for coverage and ridge prediction. Retain its adverse-to-proposed family-disjoint split; it is a whole-pipeline comparator, not a matched predictor control.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Historical per-benchmark mean\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Provides a simple historical-only reference. Fixed-audit errors are available in observation records; primary remaining-target errors use the proposed policy’s residual target sets and must be described accordingly.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Historical-column permutation, queried-score average, and reset replay\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Permutation preserves recorded masks and column marginals while disrupting cross-task alignment; the queried-score-average control tests a deliberately weak score-copying alternative; replay checks deterministic reset behavior. These are distinct controls, not independent methods or evidence that any one proposed component is necessary. Keep the queried-score average and replay detail in the supplement.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Lead with final-evaluation comparisons only: final.metric.candidate_mae=0.031296949105441715, final.metric.greedy_ridge_mae=0.036070484115358764, final.metric.random_lowrank_mae=0.04563401706946738, and final.metric.historical_mean_mae=0.08782454238980078. Report normalized MAE, lower is better; do not promote the search objective into a reader-facing metric.\",\n    \"Make the common-target view central: eight panel-level audit columns are never queryable, with at least four recorded audit cells per held-out row. Derive fixed-audit MAE by method, budget, split condition, and seed from final.evidence.episodes; retain the evaluator’s row versus family weighting. Descriptive all-condition audit means derived from recorded case summaries are 0.02687114697690909 for the proposed procedure, 0.029409541552239845 for greedy ridge, and 0.0354685223509185 for random low-rank. Label these as derived summaries, not extra runs.\",\n    \"Use k in {1,2,4,8} to answer the budget question without claiming a universally sufficient k or a threshold never specified in the artifacts. Show mean curves and all three frozen split-seed values, not invented significance intervals.\",\n    \"Expose heterogeneity in the main text and a six-row split-by-seed comparison table: in the family-disjoint seed 4021, proposed remaining-target MAE is 0.03665795350948428 versus greedy ridge 0.03414373962951742; fixed-audit MAE is 0.02971584244781457 versus greedy ridge 0.026874009322976657. These are evidence of nonuniform comparative performance, not a diagnosed validity failure.\",\n    \"Original versus column-permuted historical data provides a dependence diagnostic, not a matched mixture/adaptivity ablation. Final reported proposed permuted MAE is 0.08976440453371944 versus original 0.031296949105441715. Use common-target records for the control figure; reserve the reported remaining-target permutation gap for a clearly defined descriptive table or text statement.\",\n    \"Qualification comes only from final.qualification.*: status completed, outcome qualified, correct true. Preserve this separate provenance statement and never infer it from search records. In any later audit/revision, retain a reported not_qualified outcome and all limitations if authoritative inputs change, without guessing the reason from metrics.\",\n    \"Do not include the Gaussian, mixture, median/mean, blind/adaptive, or quadrature branches as measured main-paper ablations: external paired component results and actual internal choices are unavailable. No original-incumbent final comparison is supported; the two directly evaluated native comparators remain valid.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Figure 1, early on page 1 or 2: one conceptual overview of a masked historical matrix, historical-only latent fitting and model choice, a query/condition loop, and prediction/scoring on unqueried recorded cells, with a visually distinct never-queryable audit boundary.\",\n    \"Figure 2, primary results: two-panel fixed-audit MAE versus query budget, one panel per split condition, comparing the frozen procedure, greedy ridge, and random low-rank; show the three split-seed values and their descriptive means.\",\n    \"Figure 3, dependence diagnostic: two-panel original-versus-column-permuted history comparison on the same fixed audit targets, one panel per split condition, for the same three pipelines; retain individual split-seed points.\",\n    \"Main tables, not extra figures: a four-method aggregate remaining-target/common-audit comparison and a six-row split-by-seed proposed-versus-ridge comparison that makes the adverse split visible. Complete checkpoint/control tables belong after references.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"problem_and_evidence_overview\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Make the retrospective masked-matrix object, historical-only fitting, small-budget query/condition loop, Gaussian fallback, and never-queryable audit distinction instantly legible without suggesting a new neural architecture.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"scientific objects and information boundaries\",\n      \"metric\": \"none; schematic with no invented score values or quantitative encoding\",\n      \"split_by\": \"fit information versus query information versus withheld evaluation\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"paper.sty specifies one 5.5-inch text column and a 9-inch text height, not a two-column layout. Use the full 5.5-inch text width at about 2.1 inches high, roughly one quarter of a body page including caption, early on page 1 or 2. Four or five large visual units with at least 9-point final labels remain readable; a 0.48-width placement would make the information boundary text too small.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 1,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"source_paths\": [\n        \"task/task_contract.json\",\n        \"task/evaluate_final.py\",\n        \"results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/program.py\",\n        \"results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/mixture.py\"\n      ],\n      \"export_requirements\": \"Keep the editable draw.io source and vector PDF; export a 300-DPI PNG companion and reference PDF in LaTeX. If only raster is available, regenerate at at least 2400 pixels wide; never upscale.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"common_target_budget_comparison\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Compare useful small-budget prediction on unchanged audit targets and show between-split variability without conflating removal of queried targets with predictive improvement.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"point_line\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"acquisition and prediction pipeline\",\n      \"metric\": \"fixed-audit normalized MAE, lower is better\",\n      \"split_by\": \"model-group versus name-proxy-family-disjoint holdout\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Use two side-by-side panels across the inspected single 5.5-inch text width, each about 2.5 inches wide, at a total height near 2.5 inches plus caption, about one third of a body page. Four discrete budget ticks and three method colors fit with 9-point labels and one shared legend. Do not shrink to a half-width float; simplify labels or stack panels if the final labels fall below 9 points.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 4,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"aggregation\": \"Exclude replay and permuted arms. At each checkpoint aggregate observations.audit_mae or baselines[method].audit_mae across rows; model-group uses equal row weights, family-disjoint uses equal family means; then average equally over the three seeds. Plot the three aggregate seed values as unconnected small points in the method color and one descriptive mean curve, not as additional method series. The horizontal axis is evaluation budget, not campaign progress.\",\n      \"export_requirements\": \"Export PDF and PNG from the same plotting source, PNG at 300 DPI; LaTeX must use PDF. Raster-only fallback must be regenerated at at least 2400 pixels wide.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"historical_alignment_control\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Show whether the evaluated prediction pipelines rely on historical cross-task alignment while maintaining common held-out targets and identifying the control’s non-causal limitations.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"paired_dot\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"proposed, greedy ridge, and random low-rank pipelines\",\n      \"metric\": \"fixed-audit normalized MAE averaged equally over the recorded query budgets\",\n      \"split_by\": \"model-group versus name-proxy-family-disjoint holdout; original versus column-permuted historical scores\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Two side-by-side panels use the full single-column 5.5-inch text width, about 2.2 inches high plus caption, roughly one quarter to one third of a page. Three abbreviated method categories and two arm styles per panel leave room for 9-point labels, visible raw split-seed points, and a common y scale. No inset or third cramped panel is needed.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 2,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"aggregation\": \"Use each non-replay case.audit_mae for the proposed pipeline and case.baseline_audit in documented order [random_lowrank, greedy_ridge]. These already average checkpoints with the appropriate row/family weighting. Pair original and permuted points within seed and condition; show all seeds and descriptive means. This refits/tunes the complete pipeline on permuted history and is not a pure covariance-only, mixture-only, or adaptivity-only ablation.\",\n      \"export_requirements\": \"Export vector PDF plus 300-DPI PNG from plotting source and reference PDF in LaTeX; raster-only fallback at least 2400 pixels wide, regenerated rather than enlarged.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"supplementary_plan\": {\n    \"purpose\": \"After references, provide complete checkpoint evidence, faithful implementation and data provenance, and control details needed for reproduction, while retaining the adverse split, common-target audit, absent component ablations, and qualification limitations in the main paper.\",\n    \"planned_subsections\": [\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Complete Budget-Resolved Prediction Errors\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Tables for all recorded seeds, both split conditions, four budgets, proposed and native baseline errors, and historical-mean/queried-score-average controls, for both remaining-recorded-target and fixed-audit MAE. Separate original/permuted arms and omit replay from every performance aggregate. Generate these tables directly from final.evidence.episodes rather than rerunning experiments.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n          \"final.metric.candidate_mae\",\n          \"final.metric.greedy_ridge_mae\",\n          \"final.metric.random_lowrank_mae\",\n          \"final.metric.historical_mean_mae\",\n          \"final.metric.observed_copy_mae\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Aggregation and Common-Target Comparisons\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Document equal checkpoint weighting, equal model-row weighting for model-group, equal proxy-family means for family-disjoint, and equal case weighting over final seeds and conditions. Include seed-level paired deltas and fixed-audit versus policy-dependent remaining-target comparisons; distinguish descriptive split variability from independent-run confidence intervals.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n        ],\n        \"source_paths\": [\n          \"task/evaluate_final.py\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Observed-Data Inference and Historical Model Choice\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Reproduce only the implemented observed-data factor EM, low-rank-plus-diagonal residual model, frozen-loadings latent-mixture option, conditional updates, bounded posterior median, covariance/square-root transfer heuristics, candidate hyperparameters, historical row-heldout fixed-target validation, and fallback. K=1 is the original Gaussian path, not evidence that multi-component inference ran. The implemented validation margin max(0.0001, 0.5 paired SE) is a selection heuristic, not a significance test. No measured component-ablation table exists.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [],\n        \"source_paths\": [\n          \"results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/covariance.py\",\n          \"results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/mixture.py\",\n          \"results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/METHOD.md\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Frozen Data, Score Contexts, and Reproduction\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Document the pinned MTEB revision, source JSON and resource checksums, full task/language/split/primary-metric/dataset-revision column keys, fixed metric transforms, natural missingness, content/identity separation, name-based family proxies, masks determining query availability, seeds, and exact final data plans. Provide the existing program interface and dependency files, and distinguish recorded evaluator timing from unmeasured method-only compute or cost savings. Explain final-evidence hash linkage; anonymize all identifying absolute paths in public material.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n          \"final.qualification.status\",\n          \"final.qualification.outcome\"\n        ],\n        \"source_paths\": [\n          \"task/task_contract.json\",\n          \"task/resources/final/manifest.json\",\n          \"task/resources/final/dataset.json\",\n          \"task/requirements.txt\",\n          \"/workspace/final_evaluation/final_manifest.json\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Permutation and Reset Controls\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Show all recorded original/permuted control summaries and the zero-weight deterministic reset replay, documenting mask/marginal preservation and the difference between a protocol control and predictive evidence. Report qualification exactly as completed/qualified for these inputs; it does not certify unseen-model generalization or prove absence of every validity concern.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n          \"final.metric.permuted_mae\",\n          \"final.metric.permutation_gap\",\n          \"final.qualification.status\",\n          \"final.qualification.outcome\",\n          \"final.qualification.correct\"\n        ],\n        \"source_paths\": [\n          \"task/evaluate_final.py\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"LLM Usage\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Disclose the documented roles of automated agents in idea generation, implementation, reference-query planning, and paper framing, and disclose later writing/figure assistance only if it actually occurs. Do not invent human verification, external reading, experiments, or a model identity absent from the logs.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [],\n        \"source_paths\": [\n          \"global_memory/memory.jsonl\",\n          \"memory.md\",\n          \"reference_query_plan.md\",\n          \"paper_strategy.md\"\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"forbidden_headings\": [\n    \"Search Progression\",\n    \"Campaign Timeline\",\n    \"Milestones\",\n    \"First Valid Result\",\n    \"Phase One / Phase Two\",\n    \"Iteration History\",\n    \"Experiment A / Experiment B\",\n    \"From Gaussian to Mixture to Quadrature\",\n    \"Architecture Improvements\",\n    \"State-of-the-Art Benchmark Compression\"\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_phrases\": [\n    \"we then tried\",\n    \"subsequent improvements\",\n    \"first valid result\",\n    \"next-best\",\n    \"later variants\",\n    \"the winning idea\",\n    \"the latest variant proves\",\n    \"all families improve\",\n    \"eight benchmarks are sufficient\",\n    \"statistically significant improvement\",\n    \"the mixture caused the gain\",\n    \"quadrature improves prediction\",\n    \"calibrated uncertainty\",\n    \"state of the art\",\n    \"correctness rate\",\n    \"correct and eligible\",\n    \"eligible\",\n    \"combined_score\",\n    \"rubric\",\n    \"worker_01\",\n    \"worker_02\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_writer\": [\n    \"Use anonymous ICLR 2026 and the provided single-column paper.sty/paper.bst; paper.sty lines 48–49 specify a 9.0-inch text height and 5.5-inch text width. Full width and column width coincide here; never apply a two-column 0.48-width assumption. Omit names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding statements, identifying URLs, and internal absolute paths from visible material.\",\n    \"Target an approximately 8.75-page body within the required 8–9 pages, excluding references; if the body is 8 pages its final page must be at least 75% full. Suggested inclusive allocations: title/abstract/introduction and overview 1.55 pages; related work and formulation 1.00; method 1.65; evaluation protocol 1.20; results including quantitative figures/tables 2.15; discussion, limitations, and reproducibility 0.95; conclusion 0.25. These allocations include floats and captions, not additional figure space. Finish with substantive interpretation rather than padding.\",\n    \"Thesis-first headings: Introduction; Related Work and Problem Formulation; Missingness-Aware Latent Prediction and Transfer Selection; Evaluation Protocol; Prediction Accuracy and Budget Trade-offs; Dependence on Historical Alignment; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. Treat split conditions as two views of one question, not two unrelated experiments or a search sequence. Place Supplementary Information after references and put a separate LLM Usage section there.\",\n    \"Scope the object precisely: public English embedding-model results from MTEB revision 6dabbcc68c28ea5f180ce7df79347b3ca9cc6383, natural missingness, and 48 context-specific score columns per frozen panel. The final plans have 48 held-out rows and 88–99 historical rows; these are protocol facts in task resources, not newly measured performance. Do not claim all models/tasks in the upstream repository were evaluated.\",\n    \"Define primary error over recorded, unqueried target cells and audit error over recorded cells in fixed never-queryable columns. No missing entry is a ground-truth target. Explain why policy-specific remaining-target sets can favor different methods, and why the common-target audit is essential, not supplementary-only.\",\n    \"Preserve evaluator aggregation exactly: equal checkpoint means, row means in model-group, equal name-proxy-family means in family-disjoint, then equal weighting of original non-replay cases across seeds and conditions. baseline_audit is ordered [random_lowrank, greedy_ridge]; case.losses is [proposed, historical_mean, queried_score_average, random_lowrank, greedy_ridge]. Reconstruct final metric aggregates as a local consistency check before plotting.\",\n    \"Report observed between-split variation using the three final seeds 4001, 4009, and 4021. They may reuse models or families and are not independent population draws or pure optimization-seed replicates. Raw seed points and descriptive spread are supported; do not invent confidence intervals or inferential p-values. Do not turn the 48 rows or repeated checkpoints into independent repeats.\",\n    \"The family-disjoint seed 4021 is an essential adverse result; use exact values from the episode records rather than private memory or rounded copied text. Its lower ridge error is a performance comparison, not an identified validity/adaptivity failure. Discuss that family-disjoint averages need not worsen uniformly and the method is not uniformly best.\",\n    \"Explain the method as observed-data fitting and conditional transfer, not a newly trained embedding architecture. Training standardization uses recorded history only; Gaussian residual variance is task-specific model variation, not newly observed score noise. Finite latent mixtures and bounded medians are available options with a Gaussian fallback; actual selected K, rank, selector, fallback frequency, and mechanism-specific gains are not externally verified.\",\n    \"The selector excludes a queried task’s self-gain and uses cross-task covariance or a square-root-variance surrogate across other columns. The surrogate is not exact bounded-mixture expected absolute-risk acquisition. A constrained posterior median minimizes modeled absolute error, not necessarily real-data error. Gaussian unconstrained risk identities are explanatory mathematics only; do not transfer them unchanged to bounded actions or claim a new theorem.\",\n    \"Main comparison baselines are entire pipelines, so lower error does not isolate the covariance fit, mixture posterior, median, or adaptive acquisition. Internally computed validation diagnostics are unverified; no external mechanism ablations, uncertainty calibration, timing ablation, missingness sweep, cost-weighted acquisition, additional dataset replication, or theorem verification is available. Do not list absent experiments as completed or required main-story evidence.\",\n    \"Retrospective query availability is derived from each held-out row’s recorded mask, with audit columns removed. Make this information assumption explicit. Naturally absent scores are not evaluated, so the study cannot establish recovery under arbitrary missing-not-at-random mechanisms or prospective access to every benchmark. Costs are counted in queried benchmarks, not measured CPU dollars or equal real benchmark runtime.\",\n    \"Family labels are name-derived lineage proxies, and identity/content checks operate on the frozen resources. Do not turn them into a guarantee of biological/architectural independence, contamination absence, or population-wide new-family validity. Results concern pinned English embedding panels, not generative LLMs, multilingual transfer, ranking recovery, or downstream model quality.\",\n    \"Private-only provenance: the selected source is results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1, as identified in results/best_program_manifest.json; the quadrature extension is not the frozen studied method. No downstream_study or study.* sources are supplied, so omit search winner narratives, search-to-final gain arithmetic, original-incumbent performance comparisons, and scored/unscored attempt counts.\",\n    \"Private-only qualification provenance: final.qualification.status=completed, final.qualification.outcome=qualified, final.qualification.correct=true. This independently describes the final evaluation; it must not be inferred from search status or used as scientific evidence of superiority. If a later authoritative evaluation is not qualified, explicitly preserve that fact in writing, review, audit, and revisions; unknown gate diagnostics stay unknown.\",\n    \"At inspection, references.bib and references_provenance.json exist and agree on a retrieved method-oriented pool, but essential MTEB and several task-neighborhood starter titles are not present. Metadata retrieval is not evidence that a paper’s findings were read. Prefer a small relevant subset only after permitted original-source verification; safely obtain missing explicitly allowed starter sources before final drafting, without broad domain discovery or citation chasing. Do not use irrelevant retrieved references as padding or claim novelty priority from this incomplete pool. This stage performs no external retrieval.\",\n    \"Use final.* source IDs for measured claims. Supplementary entries with empty source_ids are implementation or process exposition grounded by their explicit source_paths, not invented quantitative source IDs. Treat final.evidence.episodes as the parent source for derived condition/checkpoint tables and record exact JSON pointers and aggregation code in downstream claim/figure mapping.\",\n    \"Plan all quantitative figures as source-generated vector PDF plus 300-DPI PNG, and use PDF from LaTeX when available. Full-width raster-only figures require at least 2400 pixels; a genuinely narrower column-width raster in a different format requires at least 1200 pixels. Never upscale; regenerate from source. Record final placement, width fraction, and minimum rendered font in the downstream figure manifest.\",\n    \"Only the early overview uses generate-concept-figure; keep its editable draw.io source and deterministic content plan. No fabricated heatmap scores, architecture blocks, or effect-size arrows. Required figures have at most two panels per row, at most six categories, and at most three quantitative method series; labels should remain at least 9 points at final size.\",\n    \"Do not draft paper.tex or claims_grounding.json, generate figures, run the evaluator, or run final-paper validation in this framing stage. A local JSON/source-ID/figure-budget consistency check of the strategy is permitted and distinct from final-paper validation.\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
