{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,210p' .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md; find .codex -name AGENTS.md; command -v apply_patch || true; jq 'keys' paper_data.json; jq '{title:.recommended_title,thesis:.central_thesis,supplementary_plan,prior_paper_story_status,edit_framing_decision,figure_specs}' paper_strategy.json; sed -n '1,115p' paper_strategy.md; rg -n 'textwidth|textheight|column|5.5|9 in' paper.sty; ls /workspace\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,210p' .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md; find .codex -name AGENTS.md; command -v apply_patch || true; jq 'keys' paper_data.json; jq '{title:.recommended_title,thesis:.central_thesis,supplementary_plan,prior_paper_story_status,edit_framing_decision,figure_specs}' paper_strategy.json; sed -n '1,115p' paper_strategy.md; rg -n 'textwidth|textheight|column|5.5|9 in' paper.sty; ls /workspace\"","aggregated_output":"[\n  \"ablation_studies\",\n  \"all_programs\",\n  \"best_any\",\n  \"best_correct\",\n  \"evidence_sources\",\n  \"final_evaluation\",\n  \"generated_at\",\n  \"grounding_sources\",\n  \"idea_titles\",\n  \"improvement_trace\",\n  \"paper_profile\",\n  \"presentation_contract\",\n  \"results_dir\",\n  \"top_programs\",\n  \"totals\"\n]\n{\n  \"title\": \"Missingness-Aware Latent Models for Budgeted Embedding Benchmark Prediction\",\n  \"thesis\": \"On the evaluated English MTEB panels, the frozen latent-model procedure has lower aggregate prediction error than the evaluated random-acquisition low-rank and greedy-coverage ridge pipelines, including on never-queryable audit targets, but the advantage is not uniform across held-out splits and does not isolate the contribution of mixtures or value-adaptive acquisition.\",\n  \"supplementary_plan\": {\n    \"purpose\": \"After references, provide complete checkpoint evidence, faithful implementation and data provenance, and control details needed for reproduction, while retaining the adverse split, common-target audit, absent component ablations, and qualification limitations in the main paper.\",\n    \"planned_subsections\": [\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Complete Budget-Resolved Prediction Errors\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Tables for all recorded seeds, both split conditions, four budgets, proposed and native baseline errors, and historical-mean/queried-score-average controls, for both remaining-recorded-target and fixed-audit MAE. Separate original/permuted arms and omit replay from every performance aggregate. Generate these tables directly from final.evidence.episodes rather than rerunning experiments.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n          \"final.metric.candidate_mae\",\n          \"final.metric.greedy_ridge_mae\",\n          \"final.metric.random_lowrank_mae\",\n          \"final.metric.historical_mean_mae\",\n          \"final.metric.observed_copy_mae\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Aggregation and Common-Target Comparisons\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Document equal checkpoint weighting, equal model-row weighting for model-group, equal proxy-family means for family-disjoint, and equal case weighting over final seeds and conditions. Include seed-level paired deltas and fixed-audit versus policy-dependent remaining-target comparisons; distinguish descriptive split variability from independent-run confidence intervals.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n        ],\n        \"source_paths\": [\n          \"task/evaluate_final.py\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Observed-Data Inference and Historical Model Choice\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Reproduce only the implemented observed-data factor EM, low-rank-plus-diagonal residual model, frozen-loadings latent-mixture option, conditional updates, bounded posterior median, covariance/square-root transfer heuristics, candidate hyperparameters, historical row-heldout fixed-target validation, and fallback. K=1 is the original Gaussian path, not evidence that multi-component inference ran. The implemented validation margin max(0.0001, 0.5 paired SE) is a selection heuristic, not a significance test. No measured component-ablation table exists.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [],\n        \"source_paths\": [\n          \"results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/covariance.py\",\n          \"results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/mixture.py\",\n          \"results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/METHOD.md\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Frozen Data, Score Contexts, and Reproduction\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Document the pinned MTEB revision, source JSON and resource checksums, full task/language/split/primary-metric/dataset-revision column keys, fixed metric transforms, natural missingness, content/identity separation, name-based family proxies, masks determining query availability, seeds, and exact final data plans. Provide the existing program interface and dependency files, and distinguish recorded evaluator timing from unmeasured method-only compute or cost savings. Explain final-evidence hash linkage; anonymize all identifying absolute paths in public material.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n          \"final.qualification.status\",\n          \"final.qualification.outcome\"\n        ],\n        \"source_paths\": [\n          \"task/task_contract.json\",\n          \"task/resources/final/manifest.json\",\n          \"task/resources/final/dataset.json\",\n          \"task/requirements.txt\",\n          \"/workspace/final_evaluation/final_manifest.json\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Permutation and Reset Controls\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Show all recorded original/permuted control summaries and the zero-weight deterministic reset replay, documenting mask/marginal preservation and the difference between a protocol control and predictive evidence. Report qualification exactly as completed/qualified for these inputs; it does not certify unseen-model generalization or prove absence of every validity concern.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n          \"final.metric.permuted_mae\",\n          \"final.metric.permutation_gap\",\n          \"final.qualification.status\",\n          \"final.qualification.outcome\",\n          \"final.qualification.correct\"\n        ],\n        \"source_paths\": [\n          \"task/evaluate_final.py\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"LLM Usage\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Disclose the documented roles of automated agents in idea generation, implementation, reference-query planning, and paper framing, and disclose later writing/figure assistance only if it actually occurs. Do not invent human verification, external reading, experiments, or a model identity absent from the logs.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [],\n        \"source_paths\": [\n          \"global_memory/memory.jsonl\",\n          \"memory.md\",\n          \"reference_query_plan.md\",\n          \"paper_strategy.md\"\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Not an edit-mode run: previous_paper, staged new/old programs, aggregated, extra_tasks, contributions.md, and instantiated edit-context files are absent. Rejected means no inherited manuscript story is adopted, not that an existing manuscript was evaluated and rejected. Preserve-the-prior-story is inapplicable; broadening the source brief to generative language models is unsupported; recentering on the latest quadrature branch is unsupported. Select the frozen evaluated latent-model family, with the two split conditions as evidence blocks under one thesis.\",\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"problem_and_evidence_overview\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Make the retrospective masked-matrix object, historical-only fitting, small-budget query/condition loop, Gaussian fallback, and never-queryable audit distinction instantly legible without suggesting a new neural architecture.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"scientific objects and information boundaries\",\n      \"metric\": \"none; schematic with no invented score values or quantitative encoding\",\n      \"split_by\": \"fit information versus query information versus withheld evaluation\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"paper.sty specifies one 5.5-inch text column and a 9-inch text height, not a two-column layout. Use the full 5.5-inch text width at about 2.1 inches high, roughly one quarter of a body page including caption, early on page 1 or 2. Four or five large visual units with at least 9-point final labels remain readable; a 0.48-width placement would make the information boundary text too small.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 1,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"source_paths\": [\n        \"task/task_contract.json\",\n        \"task/evaluate_final.py\",\n        \"results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/program.py\",\n        \"results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/mixture.py\"\n      ],\n      \"export_requirements\": \"Keep the editable draw.io source and vector PDF; export a 300-DPI PNG companion and reference PDF in LaTeX. If only raster is available, regenerate at at least 2400 pixels wide; never upscale.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"common_target_budget_comparison\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Compare useful small-budget prediction on unchanged audit targets and show between-split variability without conflating removal of queried targets with predictive improvement.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"point_line\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"acquisition and prediction pipeline\",\n      \"metric\": \"fixed-audit normalized MAE, lower is better\",\n      \"split_by\": \"model-group versus name-proxy-family-disjoint holdout\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Use two side-by-side panels across the inspected single 5.5-inch text width, each about 2.5 inches wide, at a total height near 2.5 inches plus caption, about one third of a body page. Four discrete budget ticks and three method colors fit with 9-point labels and one shared legend. Do not shrink to a half-width float; simplify labels or stack panels if the final labels fall below 9 points.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 4,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"aggregation\": \"Exclude replay and permuted arms. At each checkpoint aggregate observations.audit_mae or baselines[method].audit_mae across rows; model-group uses equal row weights, family-disjoint uses equal family means; then average equally over the three seeds. Plot the three aggregate seed values as unconnected small points in the method color and one descriptive mean curve, not as additional method series. The horizontal axis is evaluation budget, not campaign progress.\",\n      \"export_requirements\": \"Export PDF and PNG from the same plotting source, PNG at 300 DPI; LaTeX must use PDF. Raster-only fallback must be regenerated at at least 2400 pixels wide.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"historical_alignment_control\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Show whether the evaluated prediction pipelines rely on historical cross-task alignment while maintaining common held-out targets and identifying the control’s non-causal limitations.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"paired_dot\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"proposed, greedy ridge, and random low-rank pipelines\",\n      \"metric\": \"fixed-audit normalized MAE averaged equally over the recorded query budgets\",\n      \"split_by\": \"model-group versus name-proxy-family-disjoint holdout; original versus column-permuted historical scores\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Two side-by-side panels use the full single-column 5.5-inch text width, about 2.2 inches high plus caption, roughly one quarter to one third of a page. Three abbreviated method categories and two arm styles per panel leave room for 9-point labels, visible raw split-seed points, and a common y scale. No inset or third cramped panel is needed.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 2,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"aggregation\": \"Use each non-replay case.audit_mae for the proposed pipeline and case.baseline_audit in documented order [random_lowrank, greedy_ridge]. These already average checkpoints with the appropriate row/family weighting. Pair original and permuted points within seed and condition; show all seeds and descriptive means. This refits/tunes the complete pipeline on permuted history and is not a pure covariance-only, mixture-only, or adaptivity-only ablation.\",\n      \"export_requirements\": \"Export vector PDF plus 300-DPI PNG from plotting source and reference PDF in LaTeX; raster-only fallback at least 2400 pixels wide, regenerated rather than enlarged.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n# Paper Framing Strategy\n\n## Decision and Thesis\n\n**Status:** ready. **Prompt family:** `paper_standalone`, compatible with `write-paper-standalone`. This stage plans a paper; it does not draft one.\n\n**Provisional title:** Missingness-Aware Latent Models for Budgeted Embedding Benchmark Prediction.\n\n**Central question:** How accurately can a historical-only, missingness-aware latent-model procedure predict an unseen embedding model’s recorded but unqueried benchmark scores at small query budgets, and how does this change under name-proxy-family-disjoint evaluation and destroyed historical cross-task alignment?\n\n**Central thesis:** On the evaluated English MTEB panels, the frozen latent-model procedure has lower aggregate prediction error than the evaluated random-acquisition low-rank and greedy-coverage ridge pipelines, including on never-queryable audit targets, but the advantage is not uniform across held-out splits and does not isolate the contribution of mixtures or value-adaptive acquisition.\n\nThis is one standalone empirical method-integration study, organized around prediction under a query budget. The two holdout conditions are evidence blocks under that question, not independent datasets or a sequence of discoveries. The observed advantage is descriptive; it is neither a priority claim nor an assertion that a new neural architecture was developed.\n\n## Evidence Authority and Qualification\n\n`paper_data.json.final_evaluation` is read independently of any downstream-study narrative. The current input has no `downstream_study` and no `study.*` grounding IDs. Therefore omit campaign winner narratives, search-score deltas, scored/unscored attempt counts, and original-incumbent performance claims. In any future input, unscored attempts remain missing evidence, not measured losses.\n\nThe current final evaluation is **completed and qualified**, grounded specifically in `final.qualification.status`, `final.qualification.outcome`, and `final.qualification.correct`. This is separate from search selection. Qualification is not a scientific guarantee of generalization, mechanistic validity, or superiority. If authoritative qualification changes during later review or revision, preserve its reported outcome—including failure—and limitations rather than explaining it away using retained metrics. Missing gate diagnostics remain unknown.\n\nThe final episode evidence is available and its local file hash matches the recorded SHA-256. Its three seed records contain original and permuted-history cases for model-group and name-proxy-family-disjoint holdouts, plus a zero-weight reset replay. Exclude replay from performance averages. Candidate-generated internal diagnostics are explicitly unverified. `ablation_studies` is empty.\n\nSource precedence is: final metric and qualification IDs for headline facts; `final.evidence.episodes` for recorded row/checkpoint/control results; frozen task resources and the selected implementation for protocol and method exposition; reference provenance for bibliography identity, not unread scientific findings. Private memory is not quantitative evidence.\n\n## Framing Alternatives and Edit Decision\n\nNo `previous_paper/`, staged new/old program pair, `aggregated/`, `extra_tasks/`, contributions scaffold, or instantiated edit-context file is present. Literal placeholders do not activate edit mode. The JSON status `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` means no inherited narrative is adopted, not that a prior manuscript was scientifically rejected.\n\n| Candidate framing | Decision and evidence |\n| --- | --- |\n| Preserve an existing manuscript with local updates | Inapplicable: no seeded manuscript is supplied. Do not invent its title or thesis. |\n| Preserve the broad generative-LLM aspiration from the source brief | Reject: the measured data are English embedding-model results, not generative-LLM benchmarks. |\n| Broaden to a shared latent-model family under two holdout conditions | Select, narrowly: explain covariance fitting, optional mixture inference, conditional prediction, and historical model choice as one frozen procedure, evaluated on one benchmark source. |\n| Recenter on the newest quadrature acquisition branch | Reject: static availability and search ties do not make it the frozen evaluated method or establish an acquisition benefit. |\n| Lead with uniform superiority or a mixture-specific discovery | Reject: a family-disjoint split favors ridge, actual internal choices are unverified, and external component ablations are missing. |\n\nPrivate provenance: `results/best_program_manifest.json` identifies `results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1`. Use its implemented procedure and fallback, not a chronology of candidate branches. Do not expose internal directory or selection terminology in the paper.\n\n## Scientific Scope and Included Methods\n\nThe data are pinned MTEB embedding results at revision `6dabbcc68c28ea5f180ce7df79347b3ca9cc6383`. Each frozen panel has 48 context-specific columns keyed by task, language, split, primary metric, and dataset revision, with natural missingness. Final plans contain 48 held-out rows and 88–99 historical rows. Budgets are `k={1,2,4,8}`; eight panel columns are never queryable, and each eligible held-out row has at least four recorded audit cells. These are task-resource protocol facts, not new performance measurements or full-repository coverage claims.\n\n1. **Proposed procedure:** observed-data low-rank-plus-diagonal covariance fitting; optional small latent-mixture prior; conditional score prediction and transfer selection; historical row-heldout fixed-target model choice; Gaussian fallback. The available single-component path must remain explicit. Code availability does not show the selected component count or selector.\n2. **Random acquisition plus masked low-rank prediction:** retain the genuinely executed baseline. It differs in acquisition and fitting, so it does not isolate acquisition quality.\n3. **Greedy correlation coverage plus ridge prediction:** retain the stronger whole-pipeline comparator, including cases where it wins.\n4. **Historical per-benchmark mean:** retain as a simple historical reference, clarifying the target-set convention.\n5. **Supporting controls:** column-permuted history in the main analysis; queried-score-average and reset replay details in the supplement. Neither protocol completion nor a weak score-copying control is a performance contribution.\n\nDo not add measured method groups for Gaussian versus mixture, mean versus median, adaptive versus blind acquisition, or quadrature. The source implements some internal comparisons, but their results are not externally verified. Do not infer an original-starter final comparison where no such evaluated comparison is supplied.\n\nExplain observed-data fitting, not a zero-filled score likelihood. Standardization uses recorded historical entries only; residual variance denotes task-specific variation. The constrained posterior median is optimal for modeled absolute loss, not a proof of real-data optimality. The square-root transfer criterion is a heuristic; it is not exact bounded-mixture integrated absolute risk. Gaussian unconstrained-risk identities cannot be reused unchanged for bounded actions. No new theorem or experimental theory verification is established.\n\n## Results That Carry the Paper\n\n### Aggregate accuracy and unchanged-target accuracy\n\nThe following primary final metrics are authoritative:\n\n| Evaluated pipeline | Remaining-recorded-target normalized MAE |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Frozen proposed procedure | 0.031296949105441715 |\n| Greedy coverage plus ridge | 0.036070484115358764 |\n| Random acquisition plus low-rank | 0.04563401706946738 |\n| Historical mean | 0.08782454238980078 |\n\nSources: corresponding `final.metric.*` IDs. Reader-facing presentation should round consistently and label lower as better. Do not display an internal search objective.\n\nRemaining-target sets depend on which tasks each method queries. Consequently the common-target audit is essential to the central thesis, not an appendix-only reassurance. Audit columns are never selectable and only their recorded cells are scored. Descriptive audit means derived from the six non-replay original case summaries are 0.02687114697690909 for the proposed procedure, 0.029409541552239845 for ridge, and 0.0354685223509185 for random low-rank. These are deterministic summaries of existing evidence, not additional experiments. Record JSON pointers and aggregation code in downstream figure/claim mapping.\n\n### Budget trade-offs and the adverse split\n\nUse fixed-audit error at each recorded budget, broken out by holdout condition. Show all three split-seed values and their descriptive means; this answers how prediction changes within the tested budget range without inventing a universal sufficient budget or an acceptable-error threshold.\n\nInclude a main-text six-row table for the two conditions and three seeds. In particular, retain the family-disjoint seed 4021 reversal: proposed remaining-target MAE is **0.03665795350948428**, compared with ridge **0.03414373962951742**; audit MAE is **0.02971584244781457**, compared with ridge **0.026874009322976657**. These are comparative performance results, not a diagnosed validity or adaptivity failure. Neither a favorable aggregate nor administrative qualification may erase this observation.\n\n### Dependence on historical alignment\n\nThe reported proposed MAE changes from 0.031296949105441715 with original history to 0.08976440453371944 under historical-column permutation (`final.metric.permuted_mae`). For the figure, prefer unchanged audit targets and show all three evaluated pipelines in both holdout conditions. The transformation preserves recorded masks and column marginals while disrupting cross-task alignment. Because the entire pipeline is refitted and retuned, this is not a pure component ablation or a proof that a particular representation captures true latent skills.\n\n### Aggregation and uncertainty\n\nFollow `task/evaluate_final.py` exactly: average checkpoints equally; use equal row weights for model-group; use equal proxy-family means for family-disjoint; then average equally over original non-replay cases across conditions and seeds. Case loss order is proposed, historical mean, queried-score average, random low-rank, greedy ridge. Audit baseline order is random low-rank, greedy ridge. Do not accidentally reverse them.\n\nSeeds 4001, 4009, and 4021 specify frozen split realizations that may reuse rows or families; they are not independent population draws or pure optimization-seed repeats. Show raw split values and descriptive variation, not fabricated confidence intervals, significance tests, or pseudo-replication over rows/checkpoints. Historical internal paired-SE selection rules are not inferential evidence about final superiority.\n\n## Figures, Geometry, and Footprints\n\nThe inspected `paper.sty` sets **one 5.5-inch text column and a 9.0-inch text height** (lines 48–49). Thus full text width and column width coincide. A two-column 0.48-width assumption would be incorrect. All required figures target the full 5.5-inch text width with labels at least 9 points after placement. Do not rescue dense panels by shrinking them.\n\n| Figure | Contents and generator | Readability budget | Placement and intended footprint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1: Problem and evidence overview | `diagram` via `generate-concept-figure`: masked historical matrix; historical-only fitting/model choice with Gaussian fallback; query/condition loop; unqueried predictions; fixed audit boundary. No invented numeric cells. | One panel, at most six major objects, one schematic series; four or five principal units preferred. | Page 1 or 2, full text width, about 2.1 inches high plus caption, roughly a quarter page. At most this one concept-generator figure. |\n| 2: Common-target budget comparison | Source-generated matplotlib point/line plot: two holdout-condition panels, fixed-audit MAE at four budgets, three pipelines, unconnected seed points plus descriptive means. | Two panels total and per row; four x categories; three method series. Seed observations use the method color, not extra legend series. | Main results, full text width, about 2.5 inches high plus caption, roughly a third page. Shared legend and y scale; each panel about 2.5 inches wide. |\n| 3: Historical-alignment control | Matplotlib paired-dot comparison: one panel per condition, three pipelines, original and permuted history, fixed-audit MAE averaged over budgets. | Two panels total and per row; three method categories; two arm series. Show recorded seed points and means. | Main results, full text width, about 2.2 inches high plus caption, roughly a quarter to a third page. Short method labels, common y scale, no cramped inset. |\n\nAll diagrams describe information flow, not research chronology. Budget on Figure 2’s x-axis is the scientific resource variable, not campaign progression. No thresholded success-rate figure is needed.\n\nCreate two compact main tables: the aggregate four-method primary/common-target comparison, and the six-row split-seed proposed-versus-ridge comparison. Do not duplicate them with redundant bar charts. Generate detailed numerical tables from evidence, not from copied plot coordinates.\n\nEvery quantitative figure must export **vector PDF and a 300-DPI PNG from source**; LaTeX uses PDF whenever available. Preserve the editable draw.io source for the overview. Raster-only full-width output must be regenerated at least 2400 pixels wide; a genuinely column-width raster in a different format requires at least 1200 pixels. Never upscale existing raster images. In the downstream figure manifest, record placement, intended width fraction, and minimum rendered font. No figures are generated in this stage.\n\n## Body Budget and Thesis-First Layout\n\nTarget approximately **8.75 body pages**, within the ICLR 2026 brief’s 8–9 pages excluding references. An eight-page body must have its last page at least 75% full. These allocations include associated floats and captions; do not add figure space a second time.\n\n| Scientific content | Approximate pages |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Title, abstract, introduction, early overview | 1.55 |\n| Related work and precise problem formulation | 1.00 |\n| Missingness-aware inference, selection, validation, fallback | 1.65 |\n| Data and evaluation protocol, baselines, target-set definitions | 1.20 |\n| Accuracy/budget comparisons, adverse split, alignment controls | 2.15 |\n| Discussion, limitations, reproducibility | 0.95 |\n| Conclusion | 0.25 |\n\n48:\\textheight 9.0 true in       % Height of text (including footnotes & figures)\n49:\\textwidth 5.5 true in        % Width of text line.\n83:\\def\\@maketitle{\\vbox{\\hsize\\textwidth\n170:% Less leading in most fonts (due to the narrow columns)\n215:\\textheight=#5\\vbadness=10000\\vfuzz=120ex\\overfullrule=0pt%\n216:\\global\\setbox\\iclrrulerbox=\\vbox to \\textheight{%\n217:{\\parskip=0pt\\hfuzz=150em\\cv@boxheight=\\textheight\n229:\\def\\iclrruler#1{\\makevruler[12pt][#1][1][3][0.993\\textheight]\\usebox{\\iclrrulerbox}}\n232:\\iclrruleroffset=\\textheight\n245:% \\colorbox[rgb]{0.7,0.7,0.7}{\\small \\parbox[b][\\textheight]{0.1cm}{}}}\nfinal_evaluation\ninvestigation\nmetrics\npaper\npipeline_events.jsonl\ntask\ntask_drafting\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"jq '{keys:keys,final_keys:(.final_evaluation|keys),study_present:has(\\\"downstream_study\\\")}' paper_data.json; jq '.supplementary_plan' paper_strategy.json; jq '{recommended_title,central_question,central_thesis,prior_paper_story_status,edit_framing_decision}' paper_strategy.json; sed -n '1,130p' .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md; rg -n 'source_ids|Implementation|supplement|Supplement|empty|qualification' paper_strategy.md; find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -name AGENTS.md; ls /usr/local/bin | head\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"jq '{keys:keys,final_keys:(.final_evaluation|keys),study_present:has(\\\"downstream_study\\\")}' paper_data.json; jq '.supplementary_plan' paper_strategy.json; jq '{recommended_title,central_question,central_thesis,prior_paper_story_status,edit_framing_decision}' paper_strategy.json; sed -n '1,130p' .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md; rg -n 'source_ids|Implementation|supplement|Supplement|empty|qualification' paper_strategy.md; find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -name AGENTS.md; ls /usr/local/bin | head\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"keys\": [\n    \"ablation_studies\",\n    \"all_programs\",\n    \"best_any\",\n    \"best_correct\",\n    \"evidence_sources\",\n    \"final_evaluation\",\n    \"generated_at\",\n    \"grounding_sources\",\n    \"idea_titles\",\n    \"improvement_trace\",\n    \"paper_profile\",\n    \"presentation_contract\",\n    \"results_dir\",\n    \"top_programs\",\n    \"totals\"\n  ],\n  \"final_keys\": [\n    \"correct\",\n    \"evaluation_evidence\",\n    \"evaluator_sha256\",\n    \"manifest_path\",\n    \"metrics\",\n    \"outcome\",\n    \"program_tree_sha256\",\n    \"results_tree_sha256\",\n    \"status\",\n    \"task_tree_sha256\"\n  ],\n  \"study_present\": false\n}\n{\n  \"purpose\": \"After references, provide complete checkpoint evidence, faithful implementation and data provenance, and control details needed for reproduction, while retaining the adverse split, common-target audit, absent component ablations, and qualification limitations in the main paper.\",\n  \"planned_subsections\": [\n    {\n      \"heading\": \"Complete Budget-Resolved Prediction Errors\",\n      \"grounded_content\": \"Tables for all recorded seeds, both split conditions, four budgets, proposed and native baseline errors, and historical-mean/queried-score-average controls, for both remaining-recorded-target and fixed-audit MAE. Separate original/permuted arms and omit replay from every performance aggregate. Generate these tables directly from final.evidence.episodes rather than rerunning experiments.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n        \"final.metric.candidate_mae\",\n        \"final.metric.greedy_ridge_mae\",\n        \"final.metric.random_lowrank_mae\",\n        \"final.metric.historical_mean_mae\",\n        \"final.metric.observed_copy_mae\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"heading\": \"Aggregation and Common-Target Comparisons\",\n      \"grounded_content\": \"Document equal checkpoint weighting, equal model-row weighting for model-group, equal proxy-family means for family-disjoint, and equal case weighting over final seeds and conditions. Include seed-level paired deltas and fixed-audit versus policy-dependent remaining-target comparisons; distinguish descriptive split variability from independent-run confidence intervals.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"source_paths\": [\n        \"task/evaluate_final.py\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"heading\": \"Observed-Data Inference and Historical Model Choice\",\n      \"grounded_content\": \"Reproduce only the implemented observed-data factor EM, low-rank-plus-diagonal residual model, frozen-loadings latent-mixture option, conditional updates, bounded posterior median, covariance/square-root transfer heuristics, candidate hyperparameters, historical row-heldout fixed-target validation, and fallback. K=1 is the original Gaussian path, not evidence that multi-component inference ran. The implemented validation margin max(0.0001, 0.5 paired SE) is a selection heuristic, not a significance test. No measured component-ablation table exists.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [],\n      \"source_paths\": [\n        \"results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/covariance.py\",\n        \"results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/mixture.py\",\n        \"results/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/METHOD.md\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"heading\": \"Frozen Data, Score Contexts, and Reproduction\",\n      \"grounded_content\": \"Document the pinned MTEB revision, source JSON and resource checksums, full task/language/split/primary-metric/dataset-revision column keys, fixed metric transforms, natural missingness, content/identity separation, name-based family proxies, masks determining query availability, seeds, and exact final data plans. Provide the existing program interface and dependency files, and distinguish recorded evaluator timing from unmeasured method-only compute or cost savings. Explain final-evidence hash linkage; anonymize all identifying absolute paths in public material.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n        \"final.qualification.status\",\n        \"final.qualification.outcome\"\n      ],\n      \"source_paths\": [\n        \"task/task_contract.json\",\n        \"task/resources/final/manifest.json\",\n        \"task/resources/final/dataset.json\",\n        \"task/requirements.txt\",\n        \"/workspace/final_evaluation/final_manifest.json\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"heading\": \"Permutation and Reset Controls\",\n      \"grounded_content\": \"Show all recorded original/permuted control summaries and the zero-weight deterministic reset replay, documenting mask/marginal preservation and the difference between a protocol control and predictive evidence. Report qualification exactly as completed/qualified for these inputs; it does not certify unseen-model generalization or prove absence of every validity concern.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n        \"final.metric.permuted_mae\",\n        \"final.metric.permutation_gap\",\n        \"final.qualification.status\",\n        \"final.qualification.outcome\",\n        \"final.qualification.correct\"\n      ],\n      \"source_paths\": [\n        \"task/evaluate_final.py\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"heading\": \"LLM Usage\",\n      \"grounded_content\": \"Disclose the documented roles of automated agents in idea generation, implementation, reference-query planning, and paper framing, and disclose later writing/figure assistance only if it actually occurs. Do not invent human verification, external reading, experiments, or a model identity absent from the logs.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [],\n      \"source_paths\": [\n        \"global_memory/memory.jsonl\",\n        \"memory.md\",\n        \"reference_query_plan.md\",\n        \"paper_strategy.md\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n{\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Missingness-Aware Latent Models for Budgeted Embedding Benchmark Prediction\",\n  \"central_question\": \"How accurately can a historical-only, missingness-aware latent-model procedure predict an unseen embedding model’s recorded but unqueried benchmark scores at small query budgets, and how does this change under name-proxy-family-disjoint evaluation and destroyed historical cross-task alignment?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"On the evaluated English MTEB panels, the frozen latent-model procedure has lower aggregate prediction error than the evaluated random-acquisition low-rank and greedy-coverage ridge pipelines, including on never-queryable audit targets, but the advantage is not uniform across held-out splits and does not isolate the contribution of mixtures or value-adaptive acquisition.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Not an edit-mode run: previous_paper, staged new/old programs, aggregated, extra_tasks, contributions.md, and instantiated edit-context files are absent. Rejected means no inherited manuscript story is adopted, not that an existing manuscript was evaluated and rejected. Preserve-the-prior-story is inapplicable; broadening the source brief to generative language models is unsupported; recentering on the latest quadrature branch is unsupported. Select the frozen evaluated latent-model family, with the two split conditions as evidence blocks under one thesis.\"\n}\n---\nname: write-paper-standalone\ndescription: Write a grounded LaTeX paper as one coherent standalone scientific study centered on a single defensible thesis rather than the run chronology.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate `paper.tex` and `claims_grounding.json` from grounded artifacts only, with a clean standalone-paper narrative.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper step when the default paper-writing skill keeps drifting into a search diary.\n- Need a standalone scientific paper built around one central method, hypothesis, or comparison story.\n- Need to hide run order and expose only the subset of baselines and ablations that support a coherent grounded manuscript.\n\n# What to do\n1. Read:\n- `results_readme.md` — campaign-level overview of all tasks, variants, and where to find results. Use this to understand the full scope of the campaign beyond the primary task passed via `--task-dir`.\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n23. Keep visible paper text free of internal pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or `rubric`.\n24. If you need a thresholded rate, name it with one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment rate, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse one label for different task-specific thresholds.\n25. When `paper_data.json.ablation_studies` is non-empty, prefer its variant summaries, replicate statistics, and explicit comparisons over inferring ablations from `all_programs` alone.\n26. Use only numbers present in `paper_data.json`.\n27. Use only citation keys present in `references.bib`.\n28. Use `global_memory_summary.md` only as private qualitative framing; do not use it for quantitative claims or `source_ids`.\n29. If `paper_strategy.md` and `paper_strategy.json` are present, treat them as the framing contract for the final draft; fail clearly if they contradict grounded evidence.\n30. Keep visible paper text free of run-diary wording such as discovery order, iteration order, or agent-loop narration.\n31. Write claim mapping JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"text\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"SomeKey2024\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n32. Run validator:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis validator also checks that `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` stay in sync, and traces every `@evidence[[...]]` link under `--paper-dir` against the `scientific-evidence/` bundle via the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier. Every link must classify as `resolved` or `anchored`; any `dangling` link is a hard error you must fix before finalization. If paper text carries `@evidence[[...]]` tokens but the bundle is missing, generate it first with `bash ../scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir .`.\n33. During repair passes, also rerun the full `validate-paper` workflow after each fix round so the next round starts from fresh feedback, not stale assumptions.\n34. When venue guidance is present, make the title, abstract, introduction, and conclusion explicitly reader-facing for that venue's scientific questions whenever the grounded evidence supports that framing.\n35. If `./paper_authors.tex` exists, treat it as the canonical author source. Keep `\\input{paper_authors.tex}` inside `\\author{...}` when possible; otherwise copy the exact file contents without edits.\n36. Keep the template's watermark block (`% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---` … `% --- end watermark ---`) in `paper.tex` unchanged; the orchestrator regenerates it before every host compile.\n\n# Outputs\n- `paper.tex`\n- `claims_grounding.json`\n- `figure_manifest.json` when figures are generated or required by strategy\n- Optional extra figures in `figures/` (grounded only, no cross-agent aggregate plots).\n19:The current final evaluation is **completed and qualified**, grounded specifically in `final.qualification.status`, `final.qualification.outcome`, and `final.qualification.correct`. This is separate from search selection. Qualification is not a scientific guarantee of generalization, mechanistic validity, or superiority. If authoritative qualification changes during later review or revision, preserve its reported outcome—including failure—and limitations rather than explaining it away using retained metrics. Missing gate diagnostics remain unknown.\n21:The final episode evidence is available and its local file hash matches the recorded SHA-256. Its three seed records contain original and permuted-history cases for model-group and name-proxy-family-disjoint holdouts, plus a zero-weight reset replay. Exclude replay from performance averages. Candidate-generated internal diagnostics are explicitly unverified. `ablation_studies` is empty.\n23:Source precedence is: final metric and qualification IDs for headline facts; `final.evidence.episodes` for recorded row/checkpoint/control results; frozen task resources and the selected implementation for protocol and method exposition; reference provenance for bibliography identity, not unread scientific findings. Private memory is not quantitative evidence.\n47:5. **Supporting controls:** column-permuted history in the main analysis; queried-score-average and reset replay details in the supplement. Neither protocol completion nor a weak score-copying control is a performance contribution.\n74:Include a main-text six-row table for the two conditions and three seeds. In particular, retain the family-disjoint seed 4021 reversal: proposed remaining-target MAE is **0.03665795350948428**, compared with ridge **0.03414373962951742**; audit MAE is **0.02971584244781457**, compared with ridge **0.026874009322976657**. These are comparative performance results, not a diagnosed validity or adaptivity failure. Neither a favorable aggregate nor administrative qualification may erase this observation.\n118:Double-blind requirements remain hard constraints: omit author identities, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding statements, identifying links, and internal absolute paths. Keep a separate `LLM Usage` section after references in the supplement.\n120:## Supplementary Information After References\n122:The supplement adds auditable depth, not a hiding place for central negative evidence:\n127:4. **Frozen Data, Score Contexts, and Reproduction.** Pinned source revision, normalization, task-context keys, resource checksums, natural missingness, name-proxy families, mask-defined availability, seeds, dependency versions, and existing commands/interface. Sources: frozen resources, task contract, final manifest, `final.evidence.episodes`, and qualification provenance. Separate recorded evaluator timing from unmeasured method-only runtime or actual evaluation-cost savings. Anonymize public paths.\n128:5. **Permutation and Reset Controls.** Complete recorded control tables and deterministic replay; distinguish protocol checks from predictive evidence. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, permutation metric IDs, and `final.qualification.*`.\n2to3\n2to3-3.12\nautoillustrate\nclaude\ncodex\ndocutils\ndotenv\ndrawio\ngemini\nheadless\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
